## RULE 0 — NEVER PUT WORDS IN FRONT OF PEOPLE UNDER {{OWNER}}'S NAME

<!-- TODO: this is the publishing-constraint layer. It goes FIRST, before the
     mission, because agents weight early emphatic instructions most heavily.
     See BLUEPRINT.md section 4 for the eight properties that make it work.
     Below is a starting point - edit the surfaces to match your tools. -->

**{{OWNER}} owns every word that appears on GitHub. You write none of them.**

This is absolute and has no "unless it seems helpful" exception. Do **not**, ever, without
{{OWNER}} telling you to do that specific thing in that specific moment:

- Post a PR or issue comment
- Reply to a review thread — not to agree, not to explain, not to say you fixed it
- Submit a review (approve / request changes / comment)
- Re-request review, or add reviewers, assignees, or labels
- Edit a PR title or body after it is opened
- React to a comment
- Any `gh` / `gh api` / GraphQL call that puts prose on someone else's screen

**Opening a PR is the one exception**, because it is the deliverable being asked for. Its title
and body are yours to draft. Everything after that belongs to {{OWNER}}.

### Review feedback: incorporate it, then resolve the thread

1. **Incorporate all of it.** Default to making the change. The bar for skipping a comment is
   that it is *obviously contradictory* — it conflicts with another instruction, with the
   codebase's own conventions, or with something you have measured. "I disagree" and "it's only
   a nit" are not that bar.
2. **Resolve each thread as you address it.** Only what you actually addressed, and only after
   the fix is pushed. Verify the fix is in the pushed commit — check the file at the SHA, not
   your recollection of having edited it.
3. **Say nothing on the thread.** The diff is the answer. Report what you changed in chat.
4. **A comment you are skipping stays open.** Say in chat why, and let {{OWNER}} decide.

Read-only `gh` (view, diff, checks, logs, list) is always fine.

## What this repo is

This is the **{{ORG}} hub** — the shared knowledge and config layer for all {{ORG}} work. It
holds the cross-cutting conventions, the agent skills, the findings system of record, and
operational scripts. It is **not** the code; the product repos are independent git repos that
live alongside this one.

This file is installed at your agent's user scope by `scripts/bootstrap-agent.sh`, so its
conventions load in **every** session regardless of which repo you're working in. `AGENTS.md` is
a symlink to this file for agents using the portable instruction convention. When you're inside a
product repo, that repo's own CLAUDE.md loads on top of this one.

**The hub lives at the path in `${{HUB_ENV_VAR}}`.** Always resolve it by that variable — never by
filesystem guessing, and never by the directory name.

